  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>As shown in the figure, in an experiment to determine Young&#39;s modulus of a wire, the extension-load curve is plotted. The curve is a straight line passing through the origin and makes an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> with the load axis. The length of wire is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>62</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and its diameter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. The Young&#39;s modulus is found to be &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p><p>The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>&nbsp;is _______.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0040_f882817dcd62.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 234px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "5",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>62</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0040_b0981e509182.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 211px;\" /></p><p>From the given graph, we can see that extension in&nbsp;wire is linearly dependent on force applied. Slope of the curve&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>tan</mi><mn>45</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>.</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>L</mi></math></p><p>Young&#39;s modulus is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></math>, where,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;is area of wire.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>62</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></p>"
  }
}